<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Il Caffè</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1764">1764</a>–<a href="/years/1766">1766</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Il Caffè was a periodical produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from June 1764 to May 1766 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a>, his brother Alessandro and their circle, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>. Modelled on English journals such as The Spectator, it discussed economics, law, science and literature in a lively style. It became a leading voice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-enlightenment">Italian Enlightenment</a> and of reform in Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/il-caffe.jpg" alt="Il Caffè" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: it:Pietro Verri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_caff%C3%A8_1764.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title means The Coffeehouse. The journal was founded by the brothers Pietro and Alessandro Verri and reflected the debates of the Accademia dei Pugni, the group of intellectuals who met under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a>'s inspiration. To evade Austrian censorship it was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, then part of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. It appeared every ten days, and its original run of 74 numbers was collected into two volumes. It drew on the thought of the French philosophes and consciously evoked Addison and Steele's Spectator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Its articles took the form of reported conversations among the cultured customers of Demetrio, the Greek owner of a Milanese coffeehouse, and were meant to challenge the reader. Like the Spectator it used irony against the morals and customs of the day, but with a more immediate political purpose. Its subjects ranged from natural history and medicine to philosophy, music, ethics, law and literature, all turned towards one theme: the need for social, economic and political reform.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, author of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-crimes-and-punishments">On Crimes and Punishments</a>, wrote on aesthetics; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a> defended Goldoni's theatre against the attacks of Baretti; Gian Rinaldo Carli wrote on the cultural unity of the Italians; and the Verri brothers argued against the purists for an Italian language closer to common speech. Other contributors included Carlo Sebastiano Franci, Alfonso Longo, the mathematician Paolo Frisi, the polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruder-boskovic">Roger Joseph Boscovich</a> and the optician François de Baillou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Baretti attacked the journal, but it won favour across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and had a marked influence on Italian culture and political life. It was the most significant publication of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> period in the country. It closed in May 1766 because of disputes between Verri and Beccaria.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Il Caffè” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Austrian Lombardy became a centre of the Italian Enlightenment: Pietro Verri’s journal <b>Il Caffè</b> and Cesare Beccaria’s On Crimes and Punishments (1764) influenced penal reform across Europe, and the Teatro alla Scala opened in 1778.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
